/** Tamar section 19.6: English prompt rules P1-P7 and the example-of-the-move bullet, byte-exact (Hebrew lists are inserted by name). */
export const VOICE_PROMPT_RULES: ReadonlyArray<{ id: string; text: string }> = [{"id":"P1","text":"P1 \u2014 One differentiator, said once. State the owner's main differentiator in full once per page, in the hero or the about body, and at most once more anywhere else on that page. Every other field must add something new: a different owner fact, a consequence for the customer, or a concrete step of how the job is done. Use the differentiator on a service page only if it naturally applies to that service; if it doesn't, lead with the pain specific to that service. Each `signatureWordTokens` group may appear at most once per page. Never reuse a hero headline, or its opening hook, on another page of the same site."},{"id":"P2","text":"P2 \u2014 One scene, then the difference. Open `aboutPageBody` with one short, concrete scene of the customer's problem: a moment the reader recognises from his own home, with a specific detail (the time, the room, what broke, what happened a month later). Then show how this owner handles that moment differently. Don't restate the blurb, the trust bar or the guarantee. Each owner fact may appear at most twice per page, and the about body must contain at least one sentence that appears nowhere else on the page."},{"id":"example","text":"Example of the move (a description, not text to copy): the strongest about body in the eval named the most common failure the owner sees from other technicians in his trade (a quick fix that skips the real cause, so the problem returns within weeks). It then said in one sentence what he does instead, and tied that to his warranty. Do the same with the failure specific to this owner's trade and his real facts. Write new words every time and never reuse the example's wording."},{"id":"P3","text":"P3 \u2014 Say what the hands do. Never use the words in `genericAdjectiveWords` or `genericAdjectivePhrases`. Replace each quality claim with the concrete physical action behind it: what the owner opens, checks, measures, takes apart, shows the customer or cleans up. Write in verbs, not chains of nouns. Every sentence should sound like the tradesman talking to a homeowner standing next to him. An about-page headline must say something only this owner could claim; a service category plus a quality adjective doesn't count."},{"id":"P4","text":"P4 \u2014 Keep the subject. In `aboutBlurb` and `aboutPageBody`, a sentence that starts with a first-person verb must contain \"\u05D0\u05E0\u05D9\", unless the previous sentence already did. Never open a paragraph with a bare-noun fragment such as a licence or title alone. Telegraphic fragments are fine only in the trust bar, the sticky bar, badges and short subheadlines."},{"id":"P5","text":"P5 \u2014 Native idioms only. Use only idioms a native Israeli tradesman would say out loud. Never turn an English catchphrase into a Hebrew noun phrase. Check that each idiom's literal meaning matches the claim you intend. Prefer a plain verb to a clever coinage. Never use the tokens in `badIdiomTokens`; use the listed replacement idea instead."},{"id":"P6","text":"P6 \u2014 Match the reader's stage. Use the page's stage from 19.4. Emergency page: the hero names the moment of the crisis, the hero CTA is a call, and the form headline invites the customer to describe what happened. Planned page: the hero names the fear of a bad choice or a hidden cost, and the CTA and form ask for a quote or a description of the job. If an emergency page has no speed fact, lead with the pain and what the owner does when he arrives, not with a pricing tagline. Choose CTA labels from `ctaLabelSets`. Use `formHeadlineFallback` only when nothing more specific fits. The sticky bar always contains an action from `stickyActionTokens` and never repeats the hero CTA."},{"id":"P7","text":"P7 \u2014 Trust bar = checkable facts. Each trust-bar item must be a fact a customer could verify: years in the trade, licence number, warranty length, rating, or service area with the cities joined by \"\u05D5\". Never put the service name, a vague adjective or a work-style claim there. Three strong items beat four items with filler."}];

