/** Tamar section 20.8: English prompt rules R1-R7, byte-exact. */
export const M2A6_PROMPT_RULES: ReadonlyArray<{ id: string; text: string }> = [{"id":"R1","text":"R1 \u2014 Differentiator across the site. Put the owner's differentiator in the home hero and in the hero of the one service it fits best, and nowhere else in a hero. Every other hero leads with the pain of that specific service. Don't use synonyms or paraphrases to repeat it; any phrasing of the same idea counts as a repeat."},{"id":"R2","text":"R2 \u2014 Scene. Write the about-body scene in the second person, present tense: the reader, in his home, now. Never narrate a past job, a named or unnamed past customer, or something the owner did on a specific past occasion. Open each page's about body with different words from every other page of the site. End on a fact or on what the customer gets, never on a lesson, a motto or a principle."},{"id":"R3","text":"R3 \u2014 \"Before\" crutch. Use \"before I start / before I touch / before every job\" constructions at most twice per page. Show the order of the work with a concrete sequence instead."},{"id":"R4","text":"R4 \u2014 Sticky bar. The sticky bar names the customer's problem or the service, plus an action: problem-word question plus action, or \"tell me what the [appliance] is doing\". Never a bare action on its own."},{"id":"R5","text":"R5 \u2014 Service cards. Vary the openings of the service descriptions. At most half may start with the first-person pronoun; the rest start with the action or the object."},{"id":"R6","text":"R6 \u2014 Trade accuracy. Use the correct trade term and the correct safety action. An AC unit has a condensate drain, never sewage. In a flood the main water valve is closed, not opened. When two verbs share one object, check that both take the same preposition."},{"id":"R7","text":"R7 \u2014 Hero anchor (from Yonatan's H1 anchor rule; he owns the gate). The hero headline opens with the pain hook. The next clause carries the exact service label and the city, as written in the owner's facts. Never drop the service label or the city to make room for the hook."}];

